# A LINUX GUEST FROM AN INSTALLER MEDIUM, AS SMALL AS THE SHAPE ALLOWS.
#
# THIS FILE IS THE ENGINE'S OWN, NOT A STAGE. Every distribution's walkthrough lives in the
# sub-repository that runs it - `easyvm-linux`, where ubuntu and its twelve siblings keep theirs - and
# this tree keeps no copy of any of them. What it keeps is the smallest file that boots the OTHER
# machine shape: firmware, an installer ISO, and a disk the installer writes.
#
# WHY BOTH SHAPES ARE HERE. A microvm is handed a kernel by the host; a `linux` guest goes through
# OVMF, finds a boot sector and runs an installer. Those are different code paths in `machine.Boot` -
# different firmware, different media rules, different adapters - and a smoke test that only ran the
# microvm would never touch the half that most stages use.
#
# IT STOPS AT THE INSTALLER. The point is that the engine can start a firmware-booted guest from a
# medium and read its screen; installing would add forty minutes and test the distributor's installer
# rather than this program. A stage that needs the whole install is the stage's own file, in its own
# repository.
#
# WHAT IT EXERCISES:
#
#	`needs:`      the ISO is on disk before the guest starts
#	`boot` + `iso` + `os linux`
#	              the firmware path: OVMF, a boot sector, a medium, a disk
#	`settle`      the boot really draws something rather than returning at the first frame
#	`read`        a frame through `screendump`
#	`check`       a measurement on it, in a rect that exists at this resolution
#	`stop kill`   the machine goes away, which is what a stop has to do on every shape
walkthrough "the engine boots a firmware guest from an installer medium"
resolution 1920x1080
recording_resolution 1920x1080

step "the installer medium is on disk"
  needs [ubuntu.iso]

step "an empty disk for the installer to write"
  disk disk.qcow2
  disk_gb 32

step "boot the installer, waiting up to 1800s"
  boot
  iso ubuntu.iso
  # A FIXED SIZE, because the installer's own minimum is above what a smaller guest would get and a
  # machine that dies of low memory reads as a broken medium.
  memory_mb 4096
  os linux
  timeout 1800

step "let the medium get as far as drawing something"
  # THE BOOT MENU IS NOT THE INTENT: this waits past it, because a frame of a menu proves the firmware
  # ran and says nothing about whether the medium can be read.
  settle 120

step "read the screen"
  read
    name page

step "the screen is not blank"
  check
    source page
    mode mean_between
    range [0, 255]
    rect [0, 0, 1920, 1080]
    rect_at [1920, 1080]

step "stop the machine"
  stop kill
  timeout 120
